## 12.1 Introduction: The Kernel's Memory Landscape

The Linux kernel is, at its core, a memory manager. Every process, every driver, every interrupt handler depends on the kernel's ability to allocate, track, and reclaim physical memory with minimal latency and maximal safety. Unlike user-space allocators (which can lean on `mmap`, `brk`, and the page cache), the kernel must operate under hard constraints: no sleeping in atomic context, no fragmentation-induced failures during interrupt handling, and strict accounting so that a runaway driver cannot starve the rest of the system.

