## 12.2 The Buddy Allocator: Physical Frame Management

The buddy system manages physical memory in power-of-two blocks called *orders*. An order-*n* block contains 2^*n* contiguous pages (a page being 4 KiB on most architectures). The kernel maintains, per NUMA node and per memory zone, an array of free lists — one list per order — and allocates by splitting larger blocks and frees by merging ("buddying") adjacent equal-order blocks.

The central structure is `struct free_area`:

```c
struct free_area {
    unsigned int nr_free;
    struct list_head free_list;
};
```

A zone holds `MAX_ORDER` such areas (typically 11, covering 1 page to 4 MiB). Allocation walks upward from the requested order until it finds a non-empty list, then splits downward:

```c
